  <name><text>Ch10_P04_Ex1_Repair_the_step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>To repair the proof, the induction step must go from an odd number \(k\) to the next odd number, \(k + 2\). Assume that \(7^k + 1 = 8m\) for some integer \(m\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Complete the line.</p>
<div>\(7^{k+2} + 1 = 49 \times 7^k + 1 = 49(7^k + 1) -\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Hence \(7^{k+2} + 1\) is 8 times an expression in \(m\), so it is a multiple of 8. Complete the line, giving your answer in terms of \(m\).</p>
<div>\(7^{k+2} + 1 = 8 \times \big(\) [[input:ans2]] \(\big)\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>Why \(k + 2\)? Suppose the step went from \(k\) to \(2k + 1\) instead, which is also odd. Starting from the base case \(n = 1\), the chain of cases would be \(n = 1\), then 3, then 7, and so on. What is the smallest odd number that this chain never reaches?</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The corrected induction step goes from an odd number \(k\) to the next odd number, \(k + 2\). Assume that \(7^k + 1 = 8m\) for some integer \(m\) (the induction hypothesis). Then</p>
\[\begin{aligned} 7^{k+2} + 1 &= 49 \times 7^k + 1 &&\text{since } 7^{k+2} = 7^2 \times 7^k\\ &= 49(7^k + 1) - 48 &&\text{since } 49(7^k + 1) = 49 \times 7^k + 49\\ &= 49 \times 8m - 48 &&\text{by the induction hypothesis}\\ &= 8(49m - 6) &&\text{since } 48 = 8 \times 6.\end{aligned}\]
<p>Since \(49m - 6\) is an integer, \(7^{k+2} + 1\) is a multiple of 8. So if the statement holds for an odd number \(k\), it holds for \(k + 2\). With the base case \(n = 1\), this gives \(n = 3\), then \(n = 5\), then \(n = 7\), and so on: every odd positive integer.</p>
<p>A step from \(k\) to \(2k + 1\) would also preserve the property, but the chain of cases it gives, \(1, 3, 7, 15, 31, \ldots\), misses 5 (and 9, 11, 13, ...). The induction step must lead to the next case, so that the base case and the step together cover every case of the statement.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
